IMPORTANT: Update the deletion and editing requirements to follow this final business rule.

Mini-Tipia must temporarily lock a product's inventory and product structure whenever that product is involved in an active Sales draft or Receive Delivery draft.

This requirement supersedes any earlier instruction that allowed inventory or product-structure changes while a product was in an active draft.
1. What must be locked
If a product appears in at least one active Sales draft or Receive Delivery draft, block all the following operations on that product:

Inventory operations
- Editing stock quantities.
- Increasing or decreasing stock.
- Adding a new batch.
- Deleting an existing batch.
- Editing batch details, including batch number and expiry date.
- Any other operation that changes inventory records or stock quantities.
Product structure operations
- Adding or deleting a variant.
- Editing or deleting a packaging level.
- Adding a packaging level.
- Changing packaging conversion quantities.
- Changing packaging hierarchy relationships.
- Any other operation that changes the product's variants or packaging structure.
The lock applies to the entire product, not just the particular variant, batch, or packaging level referenced by the draft.

Example:

Panadol has two variants:
- Panadol 500mg Tablets.
- Panadol Extra Tablets.
If Panadol 500mg Tablets appears in an active Sales draft, the entire Panadol product is locked.

The user must not be able to edit stock, add batches, delete batches, add packaging levels, change conversion quantities, or delete the other variant, even if those particular records are not referenced by the draft.

Apply exactly the same rule to active Receive Delivery drafts.
2. What remains editable
Do not freeze the entire product.

The following non-stock descriptive changes remain allowed:
- Product name.
- Product description.
- Other descriptive metadata that does not affect product identity, variant relationships, packaging, inventory, stock calculations, or draft references.
Do not allow a supposedly descriptive field to be edited if it affects product identity or how Sales and Receive Delivery resolve product references.

If uncertain whether a field is descriptive or structural, treat it as structural and block editing while the product is locked.
3. User feedback
When a user attempts a blocked operation, explain why the product is locked.

For an active Sales draft, display:

"This product is currently in a Sales draft. Its inventory and product structure cannot be modified until the draft is completed or cancelled. You can still edit its name and description."

For an active Receive Delivery draft, display:

"This product is currently in a Receive Delivery draft. Its inventory and product structure cannot be modified until the draft is completed or cancelled. You can still edit its name and description."

If the product is in both types of active drafts, explain both restrictions.

Do not silently ignore the user's action.
4. Enforce the lock throughout the application
This must not be implemented as a UI-only restriction.

Apply the product-locking rule at:
- The relevant UI editing entry points.
- The application/workflow validation layer.
- The application-state/reducer commit boundary.
A user must not be able to bypass the restriction by invoking a different editing workflow or by bypassing the UI.

Recheck the current active draft state when saving or committing an operation. Do not rely only on the draft status when an editing modal was first opened.

The product lock must be lifted automatically when all relevant Sales and Receive Delivery drafts have been completed or cancelled.

Completed Sales, received deliveries, cancelled drafts, and other inactive drafts must not keep a product locked.

Do not automatically modify, cancel, or complete drafts to make an operation possible.
5. Preserve the deletion-reason requirements
This new locking policy does not remove the previously established deletion requirements.

When no active draft locks the product:
- Variant deletion must still be confirmed, staged, and require its own clear reason.
- Packaging-level deletion must still be confirmed, staged, and require its own clear reason.
- Batch deletion must still be confirmed, staged, and require its own clear reason.
- Save Changes must remain disabled until all required deletion reasons are valid.
- Actual changes must only be committed when Save Changes is clicked.
- Cancelling the editing session must discard staged deletions without changing persisted product or inventory data.
If an active draft exists, block the deletion before it can be staged or committed.
6. Important implementation boundaries
- Do not change the accepted Sales or Receive Delivery workflows unnecessarily.
- Do not change how stock is calculated, allocated, broken down, or built up.
- Do not introduce SQLite or new persistence architecture.
- Do not introduce archiving or soft deletion.
- Do not refactor unrelated functionality.
- Preserve existing Activity snapshots and event integrity.
- Continue recording only completed business changes in Activity.
7. Required tests
Add tests proving that an active Sales draft locks:
- Stock quantity editing.
- Batch creation and deletion.
- Batch detail editing.
- Variant addition and deletion.
- Packaging-level addition and deletion.
- Packaging conversion and hierarchy editing.
Repeat the same coverage for active Receive Delivery drafts.

Also prove:
- A draft involving one variant locks every variant belonging to that product.
- A draft for another product does not lock the current product.
- Completed and cancelled drafts do not lock the product.
- Product name and description can still be edited while the product is locked.
- Structural or stock-affecting fields cannot be changed through the descriptive edit workflow.
- The UI and reducer both enforce the restriction.
- A draft becoming active after an editing modal opens is detected when the user attempts to save.
- Cancelling or completing all relevant drafts lifts the lock.
- Rejected operations leave the entire application state unchanged.
- Existing deletion-reason and staged-deletion tests continue to pass.
Run focused tests, the full test suite, build, lint, and git diff --check.

Report the exact files changed, the operations blocked and allowed, tests added, results, and any unverified behavior.

Do not claim completion based only on passing tests. Distinguish automated verification from manual UI verification

